WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.393
- Hi everyone.

00:00:11.393 --> 00:00:14.890
Welcome to the "The Daily
Homeroom" livestream.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.140
This is just a way for
all of us to stay in touch

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.740
during this time of school closures.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.130
And as we have in homeroom
homeroom in the real world,

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.890
the physical world which
we all now really miss,

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.210
it's a time for us to come together,

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.320
to make announcements,
to answer questions,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.700
and that's what we wanna do here.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.220
So, as we go into the meat of
this homeroom conversation,

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.150
and it's really just going to be

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.500
a really informal session
today of being able

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.060
to ask myself and my
team member Dan is going

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.660
to join us as well,
literally any question.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.670
So, go on to Facebook, go on to YouTube,

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.840
and ask anything you would like (laughs)

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:50.352
of me or the team here at Khan Academy.

00:00:50.352 --> 00:00:53.370
What we have already been
putting out for all of y'all,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:57.030
parents, teachers, students,
over the last several weeks

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.070
is based on the realization
that Khan Academy exists,

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.580
we never could've foreseen
this type of a situation.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.740
But, we wanna create
structures and support

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.370
so that you can use all the content,

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.010
and all the software
that we've been making.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:09.930
Which is all free.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.100
It's all non-commercial in
a way that meets your needs

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.330
as a parent, student, or a teacher.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.020
And so, you can see right over here,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.360
these remote learning resources
we've been putting out.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.130
Things like teacher
webinars, parent webinars,

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.850
lesson daily schedules for
students of different ages.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.660
Also, we put out learning
plans recently in math

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.870
so that students could learn
through the end of the year,

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.340
and through next summer,
or this coming summer

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.380
so that they can not stop learning.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.060
I do wanna give a special shout-out

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.630
to several corporate partners who

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.770
are helping support all of this.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.100
Many of y'all know, Khan
Academy's a not-for-profit,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.160
and with a mission of providing you

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.320
free world-class education
for anyone, anywhere,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.570
and that's only possible
through philanthropic donations

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.000
from folks like yourself and from some of

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.100
our corporate partners
that you see listed here.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.390
These are folks that literally

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.180
just stepped up in the last few weeks.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.400
We have other folks who
have been helping us

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.040
keep going over the last several years,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.780
but we were running at
a deficit even going

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.140
into this situation and
our deficit increased

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.300
because our traffic now is 2.5,

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.480
actually approaching three
x of what it typically is.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:11.950
We're trying to do more supports,

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.960
trying to accelerate some
content software programs

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.900
that we normally would've
done in a few years

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.610
so we can do it sooner than later.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:23.490
Bank of America, AT&amp;T, Novartis,
Google.org, and Fastly,

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.220
all organizations that are really

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.430
helping Khan Academy
deliver on its mission.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.470
But with that, I would love to

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.690
literally answer any question you have,

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.860
and I'll issue a challenge to surprise me.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.440
And I have team members,
we have team members,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.580
who are looking at the
message boards on Khan Academy

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.600
and they are going to
surface any questions,

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.830
and Dan will also help me surface

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.443
any questions that folks might be having.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.040
Let's see, we have some questions.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.840
I could start while everyone's warming up

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.150
with some questions we had from last year.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.633
Sorry, from last Friday
that we never got to.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.967
One person Abderrahman (mumbles)
Hasan from YouTube asks,

00:03:12.967 --> 00:03:14.897
"How come Khan Academy
is running a deficit,

00:03:14.897 --> 00:03:17.367
"and what will that have
an impact on the provision

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:20.230
"of free world-class
education as you do now?"

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:23.107
That's a very good question, Abderrahman,

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.440
so, you know, we're running at a deficit

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.830
because, you know, simple
math, we're spending more

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.780
than we are bringing in in
philanthropic donations.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.260
And we are able to do
that in the near term.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.030
We do have a little bit of reserves,

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.540
but obviously that's not something

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.610
that we're going to be
able to do indefinitely.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:44.325
So, what we've been, the reason
why we've been spending more

00:03:44.325 --> 00:03:47.160
than the resources we've been bringing in,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.720
is the belief that these
things need to be out there.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.110
They'll be able to serve students,

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:53.190
parents, and teachers that much more,

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:56.350
and the hope that folks
are going to step up

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.490
whether they're individual
donors, or corporations,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.090
or foundations to allow
us to do this work.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:05.430
So, running a not-for-profit,
there's always this tension

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.040
of sometimes you wanna move ahead

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.010
because you think some work, especially,

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.670
we're a bit of a unique non-for-profit

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:12.860
that we are, we do software and content.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.870
We aren't your traditional not-for-profit

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:17.040
that, you know, maybe does other types

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.820
of great work like running a soup kitchen,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:22.600
or providing malaria
nets, or things like that.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.070
And so, a lot of our work
has to be front-loaded

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:26.740
in terms of expense,
in terms of, you know,

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.460
we have approaching a hundred
engineers at Khan Academy.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.730
We have designers, product managers

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.280
who are all thinking a year a head,

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.370
two years ahead of what to build.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.597
And so, sometimes we have to say,

00:04:36.597 --> 00:04:39.801
"Okay, let's build it and hope

00:04:39.801 --> 00:04:41.430
"that we're going to
be able to support it."

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.600
But it's a constant,
it's a constant tension

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.310
that we are trying to figure out.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.530
But my, you know, it's my intent

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:51.370
that Khan Academy,
hopefully, doesn't disappear.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.203
You know?

00:04:52.203 --> 00:04:53.036
When I do wake up in the middle

00:04:53.036 --> 00:04:54.320
of the night that is my main concern.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.020
I wanna ensure that Khan
Academy is an organization

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.320
that doesn't just last
through my lifetime,

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.290
but has the financial foundation

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.290
that it can last well
beyond any of our lifetimes

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.870
to serve hopefully billions of
folks in generations to come.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:10.663
But, that is, you're
hitting at a very real issue

00:05:10.663 --> 00:05:13.920
for me and the team here.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:14.753
So--

00:05:14.753 --> 00:05:16.175
- Sal, we have another question.

00:05:16.175 --> 00:05:17.040
Sal, we have a great question

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.760
from YouTube from Selena Chang.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.157
She says first, "Hi, Sal.

00:05:20.157 --> 00:05:22.370
"Hope you and your family are doing well."

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:24.640
Selena's been engaging in online courses

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.575
at Khan Academy and meditation
and her question is,

00:05:27.575 --> 00:05:30.977
"What are some of the
habits that she can enforce

00:05:30.977 --> 00:05:33.330
"to optimize productivity and learning?".

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.120
- So, take anything, Selena,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.580
anything I have with a grain of salt.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.430
I'm always trying to
optimize my productivity,

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:43.050
and learning as well, but
the things that I found

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.930
are definitely, some
form of structure helps,

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:48.940
and it doesn't have to be,
and I actually wouldn't

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:50.370
make that structure too intense.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.670
I think it's actually bad if your day

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.080
is completely chopped up
into very small sections

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.880
because then you don't have
time to do some deeper things,

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.350
to do some more creative tasks.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:02.730
So, what I try to do personally,

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.030
and I've talked about this
on the livestream before,

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.860
is I try to have some
easy wins early in my day.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.580
So, I wake up, I do that
meditation, I, you know, get ready.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.040
I make the bed which
I've talked a lot about

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.350
that gives you an easy win.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:18.480
And then when I, in normal times
if I'm coming to the office

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.550
or I'm working from
home during quarantine,

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.930
I try to, I find the morning is when I get

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.260
my most productive
creative work, so that's

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.327
actually when I try to do some videos.

00:06:27.327 --> 00:06:30.310
So, I try to do two, three, four videos

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:31.900
in that morning time period.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:35.130
And then I'm able to, and
then I feel really productive,

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.060
and then I try to go
into the rest of the day.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.520
If I'm a student what
I would try to do is,

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:41.900
in that morning period,
I would try to tackle

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:45.450
the subjects that maybe you find take

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.030
the most cognitive load for you,

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:48.900
and I would also make sure that you have

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.890
a little bit of time so, you know,

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.360
there's kinda this tension
between having fragments

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:54.960
of time to make sure
you touch on everything,

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.540
and then longer blocks of
time so you can go deeper.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.410
I would try to ensure
that you get every day,

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:03.370
some amount of math, every
day some amount of reading,

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.870
every day some amount of writing,

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:07.970
and then free up time in
your afternoon for maybe,

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:09.697
if you wanna go deeper and say,

00:07:09.697 --> 00:07:11.617
"Hey, how far can I get with this project,

00:07:11.617 --> 00:07:13.847
"or this thing I'm trying to start."

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.440
Other things I would recommend is just

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.070
remind yourself to just get started.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:21.610
It's easy to paralyze yourself
by overthinking things.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:22.707
But just say, "You know what?

00:07:22.707 --> 00:07:25.057
"Let's just see what happens,

00:07:25.057 --> 00:07:26.700
"and just put one foot
in front of the other,"

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:30.720
and that act of moving will
oftentimes unblock you,

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.120
but we actually are hoping
to bring on some guests

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.610
over the next few weeks,
onto this livestream.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.820
Folks who are experts in things
like grit and resilience.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.100
Folks even from the
military who are experts

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.540
at things like discipline and proactivity,

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.540
and how to stay focused on things.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.900
So, we hope to bring in
more resources like that.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:51.957
So let's see, from YouTube
Catherine Pierce asks,

00:07:51.957 --> 00:07:54.067
"How long does it take
you to record a video?

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:57.670
"Do you do anything to
prepare, or do you wing it?"

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:59.130
So, great question Catherine,

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.100
and the simple answer it it depends.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:04.530
You know, this morning I got
some messages from Charlotte,

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:06.997
who's on our content team say,

00:08:06.997 --> 00:08:10.560
"We need some new content
in some of our geometry,"

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.480
because we, our old worked example videos

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.460
used the old exercises that
we had on Khan Academy,

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:18.130
and now we have new exercises.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.810
So, for something like that where I'm able

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:21.590
to take an exercise that
one of our team members

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:23.730
has created, copy and paste it onto

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:27.050
my little drawing
program that I have here.

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.530
That doesn't take much at all,

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:31.390
and actually I try not
to over prepare for that

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:33.720
so that when the student sees me doing it

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:35.270
it's really in real time.

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:37.370
We're working through this together.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.410
What we've heard, and
at least what I feel,

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:41.860
is one of the benefits
of Khan Academy videos,

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:42.990
many of y'all are familiar with them,

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:45.220
they're not super fancy
in terms of, you know,

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.260
computer graphics, or they don't look

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:50.750
like a well-produced
movie, but their value,

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.160
hopefully their differentiator is

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:53.860
that whoever's making the video with me,

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:56.140
or someone else is really
thinking through it in real time.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.260
They aren't reading a script,
and so sometimes that leads,

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:00.630
you know, I might have a little pause.

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:02.350
I might say um, or something like that,

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:04.360
but you can tell that
we're doing it together.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:07.580
So something like a math worked
example, not a lot of time,

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:10.340
and reasonably winged, so to speak.

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:13.990
But, if I'm doing a topic that
I need to really refresh on,

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:17.260
or go deeper than I
might've even known before,

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:18.510
that could take some time.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:20.940
You know, we have videos on
Khan Academy in world history,

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.130
or certain parts of say organic chemistry

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:27.070
that I definitely have to spend
several hours to research it

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.560
and then I, what I oftentimes do,

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.660
is I might just write
some bullet point notes

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.320
just to make sure I hit the key ideas,

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:35.400
but I don't write a script.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.697
So, the bullet point,
you know, might be like,

00:09:37.697 --> 00:09:38.727
"Make sure you don't misspell

00:09:38.727 --> 00:09:41.330
"these steps of photosynthesis,"
or something like that.

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:42.710
And then I start the video.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:46.737
'Cause I do always want the
student, the user, to feel that,

00:09:46.737 --> 00:09:48.617
"Hey, Sal's really explaining this.

00:09:48.617 --> 00:09:52.340
"His brain is really with
his mouth," so to speak,

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:56.960
so hopefully people get that feeling.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:00.050
So let's see, from YouTube, Elliot H says,

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:01.470
and Elliot has been referring to me

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:05.247
as Dumbledore Sal, which
I'm flattered I think.

00:10:05.247 --> 00:10:08.767
"Having scaled a start-up,
do you envy friends who chose

00:10:08.767 --> 00:10:12.367
"less risky, more lucrative
careers in business and finance?

00:10:12.367 --> 00:10:15.680
"Does that grass ever seem greener?"

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:18.140
So, Elliot, you're asking a great question

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:20.210
that is a very deep and
philosophical question

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:24.050
because, as you know, grass
often always looks deeper,

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:26.580
looks greener on the other side,

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:30.350
but what I found, in my
life, is it's interesting.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:32.880
When I was in, I guess you could say,

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.150
the more traditional industries,

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:37.380
back when I used to
work at a tech start-up,

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:40.180
and, you know, I'm aging myself.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:41.890
You know, 20 years ago now?

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:43.440
Yeah, I use to work at a tech start-ups

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:44.860
during the first dot com bubble.

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:46.550
Then, when I was working at a hedge fund

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:48.400
after business school.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:49.840
When I was at a, and I was lucky,

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:51.300
I always had very good bosses,

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.710
and I always had I would say good jobs

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:55.020
that were intellectually stimulating.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:57.370
I felt I was compensated more than fairly.

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:00.410
But there was that, when
you are measured by,

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:02.520
if you're a hedge fund manager,
or a hedge fund analyst,

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:03.870
how much money are you managing,

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:05.500
or what was the performance of your fund,

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.350
or how large was your bonus?

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.550
Then, when you measure yourself that way,

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:12.190
it's very easy, you'll always find someone

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:14.820
who is managing a larger business,

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:18.263
who has raised more money,
who's getting paid more,

00:11:19.288 --> 00:11:22.120
and you also don't see how difficult

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:23.620
their job might have been, you know?

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:25.170
I actually really enjoyed my jobs.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:27.160
Maybe that friend of
mine from business school

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:29.020
who's making twice as much as me

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:31.330
had a horrible boss and
had a lot of stress,

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:33.630
and maybe, you know, their
relationships were breaking down.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.720
So, you never truly see that side of it.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.887
But it is tempting to always say,

00:11:38.887 --> 00:11:40.820
"Maybe I should be making
more or maybe making less,"

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:44.140
and I realized even before
I started Khan Academy

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:47.170
that that's a never-ending proposition,

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:48.480
and a no-win proposition.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:50.180
And that was part of, frankly,

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.230
the rationale when starting Khan Academy.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.420
To kinda try to be in its own lane

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.571
rather than try to start
it off as a business,

00:11:57.571 --> 00:11:58.857
and stay in that,

00:11:58.857 --> 00:12:01.810
"Oh, let me beat those people out."

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:04.020
I'd say, "No, what's the
right thing for humanity?"

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:06.360
and hopefully if we do the right thing

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:08.640
that's there for humanity that humanity,

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:11.370
or the world will help support this.

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:13.890
And, it's a very idealistic point of view,

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:15.750
but, you know, we live once.

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.210
Why not try to be idealistic?

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:18.540
Why not try to be optimistic?

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:20.370
And I'll say, you know, I can only speak

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.410
from my own narrative, it didn't come

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:24.730
without it's share of stress,

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:27.592
moments that I thought I
might have made a blunder,

00:12:27.592 --> 00:12:29.610
and I'd wonder what I was doing

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.840
to my family's finances and whatever that.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:33.310
But when we got that first funding,

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:35.390
and even though my initial salaries

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:36.870
at Khan Academy back in the day

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:39.294
were, you know, a fourth or a fifth

00:12:39.294 --> 00:12:42.250
of what I was making in
the hedge fund world.

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:45.720
It didn't matter 'cause I still had enough

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:47.280
to be able to provide for my family,

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:49.170
to eat out every now and then,

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:51.340
to, you know, have our two Hondas,

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:52.880
and a 2,000 square foot house.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:54.890
And the fact that I was
able to spend my days

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:56.660
doing something that I really cared about

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:59.670
that I was able to get feedback
from folks around the world,

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.300
form a connection with 'em,
feel like I was helping them.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.930
That made me feel like the
luckiest person on the planet.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:07.410
And so, you fast forward to today.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:09.990
No, I don't have any
regrets, and if anything,

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.960
you know, I obviously
have a lot of friends

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:13.537
who've done very very well in life.

00:13:13.537 --> 00:13:16.509
You know, I ask a lot of
them for donations now,

00:13:16.509 --> 00:13:20.050
(laughs) but I don't envy them in that way

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:22.870
because I remind myself that, you know,

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:25.450
knock on wood, you know, I have my health.

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:27.110
My family has their health.

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:29.920
You know, I have a amazing,

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.040
super intelligent, beautiful wife.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:33.880
I have hilarious children.

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:35.580
I get to spend time with them.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.450
I get to work on something
that I care about,

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.570
and I'm, you know, from my point of view

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:43.440
the board here at Khan
Academy compensates me

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:45.820
more than fairly, so I feel, you know,

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:48.070
all my basic needs for sure, are met.

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:51.460
And so, you know, what
else can someone ask for?

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:53.321
Is the way I think about it.

00:13:53.321 --> 00:13:56.660
I actually have trouble
seeing other peoples grass

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:59.810
as anywhere close as green as my own.

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:03.610
So, I consider myself very fortunate.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:04.893
So, let's see.

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:08.767
From Facebook, Laudo Stoiloff says,

00:14:08.767 --> 00:14:12.060
"How do you think students can
overcome their fear of math?"

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:14.340
So, that is a great
question, and, you know,

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:16.380
you're talking about something
that a lot of adults,

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:19.990
very accomplished adults who
are clearly very capable,

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:21.530
say, "Oh, math, I'm afraid of that,"

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:24.560
and my theory, and I've seen things

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:25.560
that back up this theory.

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.120
I saw this with my cousins when I first

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:31.360
started tutoring them 15
years ago, 16 years ago

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:32.860
that the reason why
they were having trouble

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:34.650
in math is math is very cumulative.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:35.990
And, in a traditional model,

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:38.030
you're pushed together at a fixed pace,

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:41.360
and let's say the unit
on dividing decimals.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:42.370
You're a little shaky on it.

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:44.400
You got a 70 or 80% on a test.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:45.560
Well, that gap remains.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:47.840
And then later in ninth
grade in algebra class,

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:49.820
when you have to divide
decimals in that equation,

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:51.567
you're like, "Ahhh, I never
really fully learned that.

00:14:51.567 --> 00:14:52.840
"This is stressing me out," or maybe

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:54.550
you did learn it and now you forgot it.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:57.137
And so, one of our core
beliefs at Khan Academy

00:14:57.137 --> 00:15:00.340
are we need to create the
mechanism where you, the student,

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:03.240
can learn at your own pace,
learn at your own time,

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.620
not have to feel
embarrassed about something.

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:07.810
If you're an algebra
student but you forgot how

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:09.570
to divide decimals, not a big deal.

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.950
There's many adults who
forgot how to divide decimals.

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:14.700
You can go back to that content,
and not only get videos,

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:16.790
but you can get that
practice and that feedback.

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:20.380
So, my answer Laudo is,
especially if, you know,

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:21.980
you're a student and schools are closed,

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:23.640
and you have the summer right after that,

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:26.210
go to Khan Academy, start
at the very beginning.

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:28.520
Start at early learning on Khan Academy,

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:30.460
and I know that stuff will
seem super easy for you,

00:15:30.460 --> 00:15:31.680
but you'll be able to get
through that if you use

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:33.360
the course challenges and the unit tests.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:35.860
Literally in a matter of
probably less than an hour,

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:37.620
you could get through early
learning, first grade,

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.030
second grade, and then just keep going,

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:41.700
and build that strong foundation,
and what you're gonna find

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:44.490
is when you do that, after
a few weeks you're going

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:47.290
to get to your "grade
level" and when you get

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:48.910
to your grade level with
that strong foundation

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:50.367
you're like, "What was
I stressed all about?

00:15:50.367 --> 00:15:51.540
"This all makes sense now."

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:52.830
This all intuitive.

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:56.710
We get so many letters from
25 year olds, 30 year olds,

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:58.497
50 year olds, 80 year olds saying,

00:15:58.497 --> 00:16:01.057
"I can't believe that I
used to be afraid of math.

00:16:01.057 --> 00:16:03.027
"I went on to Khan Academy
and I started learning

00:16:03.027 --> 00:16:05.317
"from the beginning and I
realized how much fun it is,

00:16:05.317 --> 00:16:07.557
"how it helps me look at the
world in a different way,

00:16:07.557 --> 00:16:10.437
"in a fun way, and I realized
that all those things

00:16:10.437 --> 00:16:12.307
"I was stressed about
were because of my gaps.

00:16:12.307 --> 00:16:13.807
"It had nothing to do with my ability.

00:16:13.807 --> 00:16:15.007
"It had nothing to do with math

00:16:15.007 --> 00:16:16.940
"somehow being a difficult subject."

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:20.560
So, that's my tip, and my other tip is

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:22.910
that if you're going to
do that, do it regularly.

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:25.120
I tell family members
all the time, friends,

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:29.330
anyone who asks me, "Put in
at least 20 minutes a day."

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:31.560
If you're able to put in
at least 20 minutes a day,

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:32.620
and give yourself even a break

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:33.900
on weekends unless you're inspired

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:35.460
to take advantage of the weekends as well.

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:37.160
But if you do Monday through Friday,

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:39.530
20 minutes a day, of Khan Academy.

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:41.990
Always progressing, always
trying to level up skills,

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:43.570
using the course challenges, unit tests,

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:46.690
to accelerate as necessary,
get that mastery.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:48.390
I think, in about a month, you're going

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:50.810
to start having a different
attitude towards math,

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:52.310
and I would say in about six months,

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:54.310
you're going to realize
that you're all of a sudden

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:56.763
one of the strongest
math students you know.

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:01.700
So, let's see, we have a question

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:04.987
from Facebook, Dede Vanwarmer says,

00:17:04.987 --> 00:17:09.170
"Do you have any resources
to make reading fun?"

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:12.330
So, reading is an area
that we are just starting

00:17:12.330 --> 00:17:15.520
to get into at Khan Academy so
it's a fairly nascent state.

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:19.350
We just launched, what's known
as a beta in software speak,

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:22.210
that's a early version that
still needs to be refined

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.630
of English and language arts.

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:26.550
That starts at reading levels roughly

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.870
second grade through eighth grade.

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:30.840
I will let you determine whether it's fun.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:34.103
I'll say the same thing
that I just told Laud about,

00:17:34.943 --> 00:17:37.550
Laudo about being able
to do things regularly.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:39.680
If you are an elementary student,

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:41.140
and reading is something that you've

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:43.510
always struggled with,
start at the beginning.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:44.900
Put in 20 minutes a day.

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:45.800
Keep going with that.

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:47.070
That could be helpful.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:49.500
For older students, we have
our official SAT practice.

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.120
It has reading comprehension
in it, and once again,

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:53.350
I think it's a mindset, you know?

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:55.120
When I used to prepare
for standardized tests

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:58.030
when I was in school, I just viewed it as,

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:00.017
I said, "Hey, let me enjoy
this thing, you know?

00:18:00.017 --> 00:18:01.400
"I'm kinda training for something."

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:03.810
The same way that you
would train for a sport.

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.260
So, that could be fun.

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:09.560
My other tip is, try to find
books that you really enjoy,

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:11.470
and oftentimes there'll be reading lists,

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:13.567
and we provide reading lists
too, that we'll give you,

00:18:13.567 --> 00:18:14.910
"Hey, here are books that are appropriate

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:16.450
for different grade levels,"
et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:17.560
Definitely take a look at those,

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:19.750
and have an open mind to things.

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:22.210
But, you know, I discovered
my love for reading

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:23.570
when I started to find books that were

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:25.570
really about topics that
I found interesting.

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:28.350
When I was young things like, you know,

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:32.730
space and the future, I found captivating.

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:34.300
So, I was willing to push through books

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:36.060
that were maybe even a little
ahead of my reading level

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:38.250
because I was so captivated by that thing.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:40.030
So, think about what you're interested in

00:18:40.030 --> 00:18:42.140
and then just keep reading. (laughs)

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:45.080
And I think that'll always
serve you well, you know?

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:47.710
The more you read and the
more you're interested in it.

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:50.010
I wish I could give you
more detailed answers.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:53.500
- Sal, would you like to take
a question on screen time?

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:55.510
We get a lot of questions
from parents about

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:57.430
what the appropriate
amount of screen time.

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:00.827
So, on Facebook, Shipra Shrivastava asks,

00:19:00.827 --> 00:19:03.247
"Hi, Sal, hope you and your
family are safe and well.

00:19:03.247 --> 00:19:05.227
"And my child enjoys
learning on Khan Academy,

00:19:05.227 --> 00:19:07.097
"and since the courses are so interesting

00:19:07.097 --> 00:19:08.547
"she can't stop doing them.

00:19:08.547 --> 00:19:11.287
"I want her to keep going,
but my concern is screen time.

00:19:11.287 --> 00:19:12.657
"Do I limit the lessons?

00:19:12.657 --> 00:19:14.467
"They're so fun and interesting.

00:19:14.467 --> 00:19:17.227
"And should the app introduce time limits

00:19:17.227 --> 00:19:18.477
"or something like that?"

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:22.160
- Yeah, great question
Shilpra, and one we get a lot.

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:26.020
And so, I don't know how
old your daughter is.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:28.950
I mean, it's a great thing that
she wants to keep learning.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:31.500
My general rule of
thumb, if she's younger,

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:34.150
if she's using Khan Academy kids app,

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:36.240
and she's four, five years old,

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:39.680
I would say try to keep
screen time limited

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:42.130
to sessions of 15 or 20 minutes.

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:44.420
And making sure that
she gets time outside,

00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:47.100
time with family, plays,
gets creative time.

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:50.960
And so, I oftentimes, I flip
the screen time argument.

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:51.837
Instead of talking about,

00:19:51.837 --> 00:19:53.330
"Well, what's the right
amount of screen time?"

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:55.350
I say, "Well, what's the
right amount of other things?"

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:58.780
And so, you gotta make sure
that your child get time

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:02.930
to play, gets time outside,
has quality time with you

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:06.030
and other family members, and
if they're able to do that

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:08.260
and they have time to play and relax

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:13.140
then I'm less worried within
some reasonable constraints.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:14.907
On the other hand, there's
kids who have no screen time,

00:20:14.907 --> 00:20:16.380
but on the other side they're also

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:17.800
not getting those other things.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:19.720
I would be far more
worried about those kids

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:21.210
who are not running around outside,

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:24.470
who are not getting that
meaningful time with their family,

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:27.460
but my general rule of thumb
for a very young student,

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:31.443
I would say maybe 20, 30
minutes at a time max.

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:34.610
You know, we're in this kind of

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:37.790
stay in place order world with COVID.

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:39.920
I think all parents are having to,

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:41.930
everyone is going to
have to get on screens

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:44.760
a little bit more than
normal even if it's to ZOOM,

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:47.710
or be on a hangout with family
members et cetera, et cetera.

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:49.890
So, I would say use your judgement,

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:51.610
make sure your daughter is able to play,

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:54.470
and has time for true relaxation

00:20:54.470 --> 00:20:56.600
and joy outside of screen time.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:20:59.520
For older students, there's
a lot of screen time

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.020
where, you know, sometimes
when I'm making videos

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:03.760
on Khan Academy which
is a creative activity,

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:06.170
I might spend a few hours doing it.

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:10.340
Or if I'm writing a paper,
or if I'm writing some code.

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:13.080
And so, if you have a 16 or 17 year old

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:16.260
who wants to spend some time
practicing their calculus,

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:19.320
or their history on Khan Academy,

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:24.210
or is writing a paper,
or wants to edit a video.

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:26.920
I would call that
constructive screen time.

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:28.380
And if an older student is there,

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:29.860
I would still recommend
that they get breaks

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:32.270
fairly regularly, but
if they can, you know,

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.280
that could be several hours a day.

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:36.180
But there, I would still
emphasize make sure

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:39.080
they have time to go outside,
play, have creative time.

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:40.720
And that, if they don't have to be

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:42.140
on a screen they can do things otherwise.

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:44.733
Yesterday, I saw my son.

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:48.350
My sister-in-law has been kinda staying

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:52.370
in place with us and
so she's in our house,

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:53.550
and he wanted to play chess with her,

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:55.970
and he like made her
upload an app on her phone

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:57.340
so he could play on his iPad with her.

00:21:57.340 --> 00:21:58.837
And I was like, "This is ridiculous.

00:21:58.837 --> 00:22:00.387
"We have a chess board.

00:22:00.387 --> 00:22:01.817
"Go to the chess board.

00:22:01.817 --> 00:22:04.110
"Do not be on your screen
just because it's a screen."

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:06.770
And sometimes we all are
guilty of that sometimes.

00:22:06.770 --> 00:22:08.460
There's something
captivating about a screen.

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:12.610
So, I definitely think, when
possible, go to real life,

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:16.203
play real chess, play
real Monopoly, et cetera.

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:22.660
So, let's see, other
questions, I have here.

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:25.227
So, Samira Hashim on Facebook says,

00:22:25.227 --> 00:22:26.967
"Can you provide guidance
to more people around

00:22:26.967 --> 00:22:29.387
"the world to set up learning
centers like Khan Labs?

00:22:29.387 --> 00:22:31.610
"Would love to see something
like it in Pakistan."

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:33.020
So, Samira, for those
of you who don't know,

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:35.220
there's a school called Khan Lab School,

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:38.110
it's officially, it's
not part of Khan Academy,

00:22:38.110 --> 00:22:40.730
but it is a school that I
started along with several

00:22:40.730 --> 00:22:42.920
other families six years ago

00:22:44.819 --> 00:22:48.010
that my children go to, and
the whole idea there is,

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:49.730
you know, a lot of people
think virtual education

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:52.550
is somehow in competition
with physical learning.

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:54.910
And, I've always saw it
as the exact opposite.

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:56.770
That virtual things like Khan Academy,

00:22:56.770 --> 00:22:59.300
could be used to liberate and empower

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:01.290
what happens in a physical setting.

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:04.400
That it could allow
students to do more games,

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:06.340
and simulations, and
have Socratic dialogue,

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:07.653
and time for play.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:11.060
I wrote about that in "The
One World Schoolhouse,"

00:23:11.060 --> 00:23:12.817
and then to prove that out I said,

00:23:12.817 --> 00:23:14.390
"Well, there's no better way to prove it

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:16.090
then to actually start a lab school."

00:23:16.090 --> 00:23:18.840
So that's what Khan Lab Schools is.

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:22.010
Our hope over time, the
school is now K through 11.

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:24.280
It's going to be K through
12 in a few months.

00:23:24.280 --> 00:23:27.660
Is that as we can prove
out this model more,

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:29.180
it's also not for profit.

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:31.110
We just wanna share it
with the whole world.

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:32.487
We wanna share what the model looks like,

00:23:32.487 --> 00:23:34.410
and so I'm hoping that in a few years

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:36.160
anyone who's interested
in starting a school

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:38.667
like that can say, "Okay,
this is what they did

00:23:38.667 --> 00:23:40.767
"out there in California.

00:23:40.767 --> 00:23:43.877
"Here, my geography, Pakistan,"
or wherever it might be,

00:23:43.877 --> 00:23:46.127
"is a little bit different,
but I can modify it

00:23:46.127 --> 00:23:48.390
"to what we're doing in my geography."

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:51.460
Because I hope it can be an
example of what could be done.

00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:53.390
Either as a new type of school,

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:55.147
or in traditional schools they say,

00:23:55.147 --> 00:23:56.537
"Hey, maybe we could run our math class

00:23:56.537 --> 00:23:57.760
"a little bit more like that."

00:23:57.760 --> 00:24:00.240
And it's not that Khan Lab
School has it all figured out.

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:02.140
A lot of what is happening
at Khan Lab School

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:03.890
is informed by what's
happening outside of it.

00:24:03.890 --> 00:24:05.940
For example, Tim Vanderberg, who we had

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:07.440
on the daily livestream talking about

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:09.610
how he's had an incredible results

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:12.360
with his sixth grade students
in Hesparian, California.

00:24:14.413 --> 00:24:16.247
I forwarded that to the
Khan Lab School teams like,

00:24:16.247 --> 00:24:18.157
"Hey, Tim's got some great ideas.

00:24:18.157 --> 00:24:20.847
"We gotta figure out
how we can incorporate

00:24:20.847 --> 00:24:22.330
"some of his ideas a little bit better."

00:24:22.330 --> 00:24:24.820
But, thanks for asking.

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:28.250
So, let's see other questions.

00:24:28.250 --> 00:24:30.430
So, Kim (mumbles), and
I'm literally reading

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:32.870
these without knowing what
I'm about to read out loud.

00:24:32.870 --> 00:24:34.667
From Facebook says, "Hi, Sal.

00:24:34.667 --> 00:24:36.127
"If you did not work with Khan Academy

00:24:36.127 --> 00:24:37.627
"what else would you be doing?

00:24:37.627 --> 00:24:40.227
"What other projects are high
on your list of importance

00:24:40.227 --> 00:24:42.850
"with regards to your
interest and skill sets?"

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:44.910
That's, fascinating question.

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:47.010
Who knows what that alternate reality is?

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:51.660
I will say that, it's
perhaps in my nature.

00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:56.250
Obviously Khan Academy is
where I focus all my energies,

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:59.750
but there's parts of the
broader vision of Khan Academy

00:24:59.750 --> 00:25:02.290
around, how do you fully empower people.

00:25:02.290 --> 00:25:03.540
How do you empower teachers?

00:25:03.540 --> 00:25:04.550
How do you empower students?

00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:05.860
How can you reach people who

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:07.310
might not have access to school?

00:25:07.310 --> 00:25:09.000
How can you give people credentials

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:10.640
so they can prove what they know?

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:13.590
So, all of those types of
problems, I'm fascinated by.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:17.090
And so, I'm hoping we can
tackle most or all of them

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:19.190
within the umbrella of Khan Academy,

00:25:19.190 --> 00:25:22.290
but whenever I meet a
friend who has skills

00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:24.437
in a certain domain, and they're like,

00:25:24.437 --> 00:25:25.420
"Hey, what should I work on?"

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:26.253
I was like, "Well, you should think

00:25:26.253 --> 00:25:28.360
"about a way to pair
teachers and students,"

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:32.440
or so that they can, it could
even be informal tutoring.

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:35.640
But doing in a way that can
be free to empower everyone,

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.010
or we should figure out a way,

00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:38.520
so that we can credential people,

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:40.490
so that more people can
get apprenticeships.

00:25:40.490 --> 00:25:42.493
So, that whole area, I
obviously, of interest to me.

00:25:42.493 --> 00:25:45.030
I would say that if I
wasn't working in education,

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:46.860
the other area that's interesting,

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:48.800
you know we talked about
meditation earlier.

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:53.300
I've become really intrigued by happiness.

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:56.320
How does someone become happy?

00:25:56.320 --> 00:26:00.540
So much of our life we
measure by economic success,

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.500
and happiness, at some level,

00:26:02.500 --> 00:26:04.630
you do need some base economics.

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:05.463
And you see that, you know,

00:26:05.463 --> 00:26:07.160
it's a scary world we're living in now

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:08.930
where even in places like the US

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:11.800
where because of the
quarantine, or the closures,

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:14.100
a lot of peoples economics
have gotten a lot tougher.

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:16.060
So, you need that baseline economics

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:18.860
to have that baseline of not
being overstressed, and happy.

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:21.010
But then, you see beyond that, you know,

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:23.760
things like wealth doesn't
necessarily correlate

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:24.700
so much with happiness.

00:26:24.700 --> 00:26:28.460
So, it's a fascinating
thing from my point of view.

00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:30.780
And, when I think about my children,

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:32.430
obviously education matters.

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:35.110
I want them to be proficient in math,

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:37.580
and reading, and writing,
and have a love for science,

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:39.210
and wanna invent things.

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:40.960
But I also see more and
more, and I see this

00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:43.270
with kids I grew up
with and family members,

00:26:43.270 --> 00:26:46.570
that you can be off the
charts academically,

00:26:46.570 --> 00:26:50.320
but if you go through, you
know, life is gonna throw stuff

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:52.190
at you and I've been fortunate.

00:26:52.190 --> 00:26:53.180
I've been thrown a few things,

00:26:53.180 --> 00:26:54.870
but I know people who
have been thrown much,

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:57.550
much harder things, and
it's all how you're able

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:01.430
to process that and your
level of resilience,

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:02.760
and grit and all of that

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:04.910
that really determine where you end up.

00:27:04.910 --> 00:27:07.350
So, let's actually a
really fascinating space,

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:09.210
and that's why we experimented with a few

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:11.810
of these meditation videos for students

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:13.030
because that's kinda the intersection

00:27:13.030 --> 00:27:16.050
about academic success but
also emotional resilience.

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:17.320
Even some of these questions
we're getting about

00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:19.080
how do you overcome math anxiety.

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:20.470
It's related to that same notion.

00:27:20.470 --> 00:27:23.410
It's not just about learning
the quadratic equation.

00:27:23.410 --> 00:27:27.250
It's also about kind of
reprogramming your mind

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:29.210
to realize that you're
fully capable of doing this.

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:31.620
There's nothing wrong with
getting a question wrong.

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:33.910
That that's actually
when you learn the most.

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:36.850
And that, you know, no matter
what life throws at you,

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:40.680
oftentimes you just have
to wait it out and it tends

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:44.710
to get better than the very dire situation

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:46.440
that you oftentimes find yourself.

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:47.490
Obviously, there's exceptions.

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:49.590
There's people out there dealing with war,

00:27:49.590 --> 00:27:51.580
and hunger and all of
that, but for all of us

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.370
who aren't dealing with war, or hunger,

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:56.180
or bears chasing us or, you
know, really hard things.

00:27:56.180 --> 00:27:57.990
And there are people dealing
with disease and things.

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:00.270
But, if you're dealing with
just day-to-day work stress,

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:02.020
life stress, education stress.

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:04.180
Most of the stress is
abstract things in our mind,

00:28:04.180 --> 00:28:06.300
and it's interesting to
think about what tools

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:09.063
can we do to make the best of it.

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:13.317
So, Kevin Rodriguez says,
"Sal, are you planning

00:28:13.317 --> 00:28:14.617
"to write a new book?

00:28:14.617 --> 00:28:16.420
"Read yours years ago and loved it."

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:17.253
Oh, thanks.

00:28:17.253 --> 00:28:18.770
Thanks, Kevin.

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:20.340
Yes, I do hope.

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:22.290
I was actually planning before all

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:24.000
of this COVID stuff happened.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:25.880
The book might be different now.

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:28.430
The original thinking
around the book was trying

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:29.937
to get a little more
into the space, you know,

00:28:29.937 --> 00:28:31.610
"One World Schoolhouse" which, you know,

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:33.197
not to plug it but since
you brought up the book.

00:28:33.197 --> 00:28:35.070
"One World Schoolhouse" was really

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:37.360
about how did Khan Academy exist.

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:40.060
What's the history of K12
education, and then given

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:43.530
where we're going and what
the economy and world is like,

00:28:43.530 --> 00:28:45.960
what could K12 education
be like in the future.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:47.710
And, as I mentioned, it
was kinda the inspiration

00:28:47.710 --> 00:28:50.360
for starting Khan Lab
School which can hopefully

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:51.820
share things with other schools

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:54.250
to think about how things could change.

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:56.460
The next, I do wanna think about is

00:28:56.460 --> 00:28:58.330
some of the signaling to the world.

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:00.930
How does someone prove what
they know to the broader world?

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:03.223
What's the future of higher education?

00:29:05.012 --> 00:29:07.070
And how does someone showcase their

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:09.760
best self and also be happy about it?

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:14.200
So, there is an aspect of
social emotional well-being

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:15.710
above and beyond the academic

00:29:15.710 --> 00:29:17.850
that I've been thinking
about for the next book.

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:19.500
And maybe it's the exact same book

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:21.020
now that this COVID thing happened.

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:22.800
Maybe all of that stuff's
even more important,

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:25.860
or maybe because of COVID a lot of things

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:27.250
in the virtual space are having

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:28.770
to accelerate in certain dimensions.

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:30.193
So, who knows?

00:29:31.837 --> 00:29:35.250
When I get to writing that next book.

00:29:35.250 --> 00:29:36.083
Let's see.

00:29:36.083 --> 00:29:38.830
It looks like we're all out of time.

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:40.880
Well I'll answer one more question since,

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:45.630
well, there's actually
a lot of good questions.

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:47.220
Whatever I don't get to today I'll get to

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:49.530
in future sessions when
we don't have a guest,

00:29:49.530 --> 00:29:52.577
but from Facebook (mumbles)
asks, "Going forward,

00:29:52.577 --> 00:29:55.387
"how important do you think
traditional textbooks will be?"

00:29:56.930 --> 00:30:00.640
I enjoy textbooks but I think
they are in a lot of way,

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:03.290
you know, a lot from my
generation who did well.

00:30:03.290 --> 00:30:05.640
We were the subset who were
good at reading textbooks.

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:07.110
We built that muscle.

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:09.050
And I think there's always
going to be a skill there

00:30:09.050 --> 00:30:11.900
because there is so much
knowledge to be had in textbooks.

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:14.580
With that said, how is a textbook

00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:15.780
different than a nonfiction book?

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:18.710
A textbook, like a nonfiction book,

00:30:18.710 --> 00:30:19.670
will explain things to you,

00:30:19.670 --> 00:30:21.920
but then it also gives
you practice problems.

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.760
And, my belief is
something like Khan Academy

00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:26.640
can give you far more practice problems,

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:29.120
far more feedback, and
hopefully the explanations

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:31.200
we give either in video form, text form,

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:33.460
or even in the exercises
when we give the solutions

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:34.820
that that's more digestible than

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:36.910
what would be in your normal textbook.

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:39.610
So, I hope that Khan Academy can get

00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:42.670
a lot more people learning
at their own time and pace.

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:45.660
And I think textbooks will
then become a reference

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:47.320
if someone wants to
dig a little bit deeper

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:49.370
for some reason they can't get online.

00:30:49.370 --> 00:30:51.260
They always have that reference set.

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:53.890
I think could be really useful.

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:55.460
So, I'm a little over time.

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:56.293
This is fun.

00:30:56.293 --> 00:30:57.610
Time always goes by fast.

00:30:57.610 --> 00:31:00.420
The questions are getting
better and better,

00:31:00.420 --> 00:31:01.720
but thanks for joining.

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:03.870
This is a fun way to stay connected

00:31:03.870 --> 00:31:06.500
in times of social isolation.

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:07.810
I'll just remind everyone again.

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:11.260
We are a not-for-profit,
and we are only possible,

00:31:11.260 --> 00:31:13.720
we exist because of donations
from folks like yourself.

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:16.500
So, think about if you're
in a position to do so.

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:18.820
But with that, I will see you tomorrow.

00:31:18.820 --> 00:31:19.793
Have a good Monday.

